{
  "updated_at": "2026-11-26 09:29 JST",
  "total": 50,
  "toyota_summary": "China's largest EV maker is in talks to buy a manufacturing plant in France from a struggling European automaker, according to Politico. The move would give the Chinese company direct production capacity inside the European Union, bypassing tariffs and import restrictions. The EU has already moved to limit hybrid vehicle imports from China starting in December and relaxed rules around what qualifies as \"Made in Europe\" manufacturing, signaling concern about Chinese competition.\n\nFor global automakers, the deal—if completed—represents a shift in competitive geography. Chinese manufacturers have relied on exports to build market share in Europe; owning a factory changes that calculus and forces legacy automakers to compete on their home turf against a competitor with lower labor costs and established supply chains. The EU's tariff and rules changes suggest policymakers view the threat as urgent enough to alter trade policy mid-year.\n\nWatch whether other Chinese automakers pursue similar acquisition targets in Europe over the next quarter.",
